{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"REMEDIATION #3 (continued) — also applied the substantive pass-3 advisories in the same pass, to avoid burning a ~20min review cycle per finding:\n - ADV5 (my own contradiction, impact .85): my new TLS requirement would have REJECTED J5's own harness, which serves plain http://localhost:2990/jira — so J6/J7's live tests would fail against the validator I added. Resolved by making the harness the intended allow_insecure CASE, not an exception: live tests set allow_insecure=true explicitly and assert the warning fires, while the reject-by-default branch is unit-tested with a non-loopback http:// URL. The override is deliberately NOT narrowed to loopback (private-network operators are real).\n - ADV1/ADV4 (G3, impact 1.0): epic AC13 (TLS + PAT least-privilege) was uncovered by any child. Pushed enforcement ACs into J6 (reject branch + override warning + harness override) and the operator-doc AC into J8.\n - ADV2 (G3, impact 1.0): epic AC7 required a MACHINE-CHECKED import contract but no child installed one. Added a J2 AC requiring a persistent import-linter (or equivalent CI-wired) contract, demonstrated by a deliberately violating branch failing locally.\n - ADV8 (G3): added a J7 AC asserting ZERO unhandled exceptions in the reconcile log — a swallowed exception can still 'converge', so convergence alone does not cover epic AC6.\n - ADV11 (E2): added a J6 AC pinning a minimum jira version with a compatible-release bound (e.g. >=3.8,<4).\n - ADV6 (G3): added a J2 AC requiring J1's merged Gerrit change number be recorded on J2 BEFORE J2 is pushed — that is the mechanism behind the epic's 'oracles written before any code moved' claim.\n - ADV10 (G6): classify_probe_response hardcodes RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES={Resolved,Done,Cancelled} — Cloud/DIG workflow names. A self-hosted DC workflow can name resolved states anything, so sharing it verbatim would misclassify a RESOLVED DC issue as PRESENT_FILTERED. Now moves taking the resolved-status set as a PARAMETER (Cloud passes today's exact frozenset so J1's pin holds).\n - ADV12 (E1): removed a duplicate operator-attested epic AC that overlapped the fuller bidirectional-reconcile one.\n - ADV3 (G4, impact 1.0): J2 contained a genuine self-contradiction — 'acli*.py byte-identical' AND relocating the very symbols acli.py:69-70/acli_cli_ops.py:34 import, with no shims. Cannot be both. Reframed the invariant as 'no BEHAVIORAL change to the ACLI transport', verified by an import-only diff (git diff -U0 shows no added/removed line outside an import statement).\n - ADV7 (T4) resolved by the same symbol-split rework: adapters/jira/jira_fields.py survives as the thin Cloud-side contract-construction site and its ACLI callers re-point at jira_family directly.\nDeferred: ADV9 (P6, generic AC-quality restatement, impact .4) — no specific defect named beyond what the above already fixed."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785291045226335001,"uuid":"faad9ecf-8155-48a0-90f3-842ba06dc1da"}